# Cloud Deployment Models

## Definition

**Cloud models** define the **deployment type** of cloud resources: how they are deployed and managed.

The three main models are **private**, **public**, and **hybrid**. A fourth, **multicloud**, is increasingly common.

## At a Glance

| Model          | What it is                          | Managed by           | Who uses it                        |
| -------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| **Private**    | Cloud dedicated to one organization | You or a third party | A single organization              |
| **Public**     | Provider-owned infrastructure       | Cloud provider       | The general public                 |
| **Hybrid**     | Private + public, interconnected    | You + provider       | Your organization across both      |
| **Multicloud** | Two or more public cloud providers  | You + providers      | Your organization across providers |

---

## 🔒 Private Cloud

A cloud environment used by a **single entity**.

**Key points**

- Evolved naturally from the traditional datacenter model: delivers IT services over the internet while keeping resources dedicated to one organization
- Can be hosted **on-site** in your own datacenter or **off-site** in a dedicated datacenter, potentially run by a third party
- Gives your IT team **much greater control**

| ✅ Pros                                            | ❌ Cons                                                     |
| -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Complete control over resources and security       | Hardware must be purchased for startup and maintenance      |
| Data is **not** colocated with other tenants' data | You're responsible for hardware **maintenance and updates** |
|                                                    | Greater cost, fewer of the public cloud's benefits          |

---

## 🌐 Public Cloud

Built, controlled, and maintained by a **third-party cloud provider**. Anyone who wants to purchase cloud services can access and use the resources.

> **Key difference from private cloud:** public cloud is available to the **general public**.

| ✅ Pros                                                   | ❌ Cons                                                     |
| --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| No capital expenditure to scale up                        | You don't have complete control over resources and security |
| Applications can be quickly provisioned and deprovisioned |                                                             |
| Pay only for what you use                                 |                                                             |
| Provider manages security and infrastructure for you      |                                                             |

---

## 🔀 Hybrid Cloud

Uses **both public and private clouds** in an interconnected environment.

**Use cases**

- **Surge capacity:** a private cloud can burst into the public cloud for temporary increased demand
- **Extra security layer:** you choose which services stay in the public cloud and which go on your private cloud infrastructure

**Benefits**

- Provides the **most flexibility**
- You determine where to run your applications
- You control security, compliance, and legal requirements

---

## ☁️☁️ Multicloud

Using **two or more public cloud providers** and managing resources and security across all of them.

**Why organizations end up multicloud**

- Using **different features** from different providers
- **Migrating** from one provider to another (mid-journey)

**Key points**

- Increasingly common deployment scenario
- You manage resources and security across multiple environments

---

## Comparison Summary

| Public cloud                             | Private cloud                                | Hybrid cloud                            |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| No capital expenditure to scale up       | Complete control over resources and security | Most flexibility                        |
| Quick provisioning and deprovisioning    | Data not colocated with other tenants        | You choose where apps run               |
| Pay only for what you use                | Must purchase hardware                       | You control security, compliance, legal |
| Less control over resources and security | You handle hardware maintenance and updates  |                                         |

---

## Azure Tools for These Models

### Azure Arc

A set of technologies that helps you **manage your cloud environment, whatever the model**:

- Public cloud solely on Azure
- Private cloud in your datacenter
- Hybrid configuration
- Multicloud across several providers at once

### Azure VMware Solution

**Scenario:** You already run VMware in a private cloud and want to move to a public or hybrid cloud.

Azure VMware Solution lets you run those **VMware workloads in Azure** with seamless integration and scalability.

---

## Memory Hooks

- **Private** = _control_ (but you pay for it)
- **Public** = _convenience_ (but less control)
- **Hybrid** = _flexibility_ (best of both)
- **Multicloud** = _multiple providers_
- **Azure Arc** = manage _anything, anywhere_
- **Azure VMware Solution** = bring _VMware_ to Azure

